  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Tear-off Almanac",
    "overview": "The hub is a wall almanac, not a dashboard. Today's decision is a full-height white leaf: the day of the month as a huge condensed red numeral, the month and weekday ruled in red above it, and two plain-language columns, Do in leaf green and Avoid in almanac red, below. Everything else (unit readings, controls, logs, maps, history) sits beside or behind that leaf on a near-white sheet, organised by ink rules and square bordered tiles the way a printed form or ledger would be.\n\nDensity is calm and printed. One heavy variable sans (Archivo) does all the work, pushed narrow and black for numerals and kept at normal width for reading. Tabular figures run throughout so measurements line up like a ledger. Colour is functional and scarce: ink on paper by default, red for the date and anything to avoid or fix, green for what to do and what is healthy, amber for warnings, blue only for water.\n\nThe one moving gesture belongs to the world: when the simulated day changes, the old leaf tears off its perforated top edge and falls away to reveal the next day. Every other state change is a short colour transition.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "A single dominant leaf per screen carries today's decision; it is never one card among many.",
      "Condensed black Archivo numerals for figures, normal-width Archivo for words, tabular figures everywhere.",
      "Square corners everywhere, structure drawn with 2px ink rules and 1px hairlines.",
      "Colour carries meaning, never decoration.",
      "Flat paper; the only shadow is on the leaf while it falls."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Meaning Rule",
        "body": "Each signal colour has one job: red = date, avoid, alarm; green = do, healthy; amber = warning; blue = water. Never use a signal colour as decoration or brand colour.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tint-With-Its-Ink Rule",
        "body": "A tint (red-tint, green-tint, amber-tint) appears only behind text and a border in its own full colour, never on its own as a panel fill.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ledger Figures Rule",
        "body": "All figures use tabular numerals, so columns of readings, times, and litres line up.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Widths Rule",
        "body": "Condense Archivo (70–85%) only for numerals and big headings. Running text stays at normal width.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Paper Rule",
        "body": "Resting surfaces have no shadow. Separate surfaces with a border or a rule, never a lift.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** keep today's decision on one full-height leaf with the red numeral, Do in green, and Avoid in red.",
      "**Do** use 2px ink rules for structure and 1px rule hairlines inside tiles and lists.",
      "**Do** keep every corner square (0px), including pins and stamps.",
      "**Do** set figures in condensed black Archivo with tabular numerals.",
      "**Do** give each signal colour exactly one meaning: red date/avoid/alarm, green do/healthy, amber warning, blue water.",
      "**Do** put tints only behind text and borders in their own colour.",
      "**Do** offer a 300ms fade instead of the tear for reduced motion."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** break the decision into KPI tiles in a dashboard grid; the leaf is the decision.",
      "**Don't** add resting shadows, gradient surface fills, or rounded corners to tiles, buttons, or inputs. The perforation dots are a pattern, not a fill, and stay.",
      "**Don't** use red, green, amber, or blue as decoration or brand colour.",
      "**Don't** condense running text; condensed widths are for numerals and big headings only."
    ]
  }
}